{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 6, :comments ["<b>In the evening you shall know that YHWH brought you out of the land of Egypt</b> — and not we who brought you out from there, as you said: <b>for you have brought us out</b> (Exodus 16:3). <b>And in the morning you shall see the glory of YHWH</b> — this is not said concerning the glory that appeared in the cloud, for on that very day, while Aaron was speaking to them, they turned toward the wilderness (Exodus 16:10), as Rashi explained (Rashi on Exodus 16:7). Rather, this is what he said to them: <b>in the evening you shall know</b> that the power is in His hand to grant your desire, and He will give you meat, but He will not give it to you with a bright countenance, for you did not ask for it fittingly, but from a full belly. But the bread that you asked for out of legitimate need — in its descent you shall see, in the morning, the glory of His countenance, for He will bring it down to you with love in the morning, when there is time to prepare it. And it is not correct to say <b>and in the morning you shall see the glory of YHWH</b> because He will precede with the gift of the manna in the morning, for what is the glory of YHWH in that? Moreover, He juxtaposed to this: <b>when He hears your complaints</b>.\n\nNow this midrash of our Sages is not the literal meaning of <b>and you shall see the glory of YHWH</b>; rather, they said this because He divided their provision into two times of the day and did not give everything in the morning. Thus they said in the Mekhilta (ad loc.): \"Moses said, when YHWH gives you in the evening\" — from here you learn that He gave them the quail in darkness, and the manna, which they asked for fittingly, He gave them with a bright countenance, i.e., in the morning.\n\nRabbi Abraham (Ibn Ezra on Exodus 16:6) said that <b>and in the morning</b> attaches to the first clause of the verse, for because they said <b>for you have brought us out</b>, He said that HaShem will perform two signs for you, so that you may know that it is He who brought you out from there: one this evening, and the second tomorrow morning. And it should have been fitting to say \"in the evening and in the morning you shall see that YHWH brought you out,\" for <b>and in the morning</b> is not joined to <b>and you shall see the glory of YHWH</b>, since they saw the glory on that very day. And this too does not appear correct.\n\nThe correct interpretation in my eyes is that the wonder of the manna was exceedingly great, for the quail — He drove them from the sea with a wind that departed from it, according to the way of the world. But the manna was created for them now as a new creation in the heavens, like the work of Creation itself. And this is what they said of it (Avot 5:6), that it was created between the suns. Therefore Scripture says: by the signs that He will perform for you today in the evening you shall know that it is He who brought you out of the land of Egypt, for He will set a table for you in the wilderness. But by the great wonder that He will perform for you in the morning, you shall see the glory of His kingdom — for who is El in the heavens and on earth who can perform like His deeds and mighty acts? For in the great and wondrous things that HaShem does, He shows His glory, as in \"to gather all the nations and tongues, and they shall come and see My glory\" (Isaiah 66:18), and it is written (ibid. v. 19), \"and they shall declare My glory,\" and so many others. And so too is the opinion of Onkelos, who rendered: ותחזון ית יקרא דה׳ — and he did not translate it as \"and it shall be revealed.\"\n\nKnow that there is a great matter in the manna, hinted at by our Sages in tractate Yoma (75b): <b>the bread of the mighty (אבירים) did man eat</b> (Psalms 78:25) — bread that the ministering angels eat. These are the words of Rabbi Akiva. Rabbi Ishmael said to him: You have erred! Do the ministering angels eat bread? Has it not already been said (Deuteronomy 9:9), \"I did not eat bread and I did not drink water\"? Rather, the bread of אבירים — bread that is absorbed in the limbs. And the matter that Rabbi Akiva said is that the existence of the ministering angels is sustained by the radiance of the Shekhinah, and so they interpreted (Shemot Rabbah 32:4): <b>and You give life to all of them</b> (Nehemiah 9:6) — You give life to all of them. And of it is it said (Ecclesiastes 11:7), <b>and sweet is the light</b> — for through it, through the light, they attain the good of taste. Now the manna is of the genus of the supernal light, which became corporealized at the will of its Creator, blessed be He (Zohar Balak 188a), and thus it turns out that the eaters of the manna and the ministering angels are sustained from one thing. And Rabbi Ishmael held otherwise, because their existence is not in a thing that is corporealized from the genus of light, since their existence is in the supernal light itself. And because of this, they would find in the manna the taste of whatever they desired, for the soul, through its thought, cleaves to the supernal realms and finds the repose of life and draws favor from before Him.\n\nAnd they said in the Mekhilta (Exodus 16:25): \"Today you do not find it, but in the World to Come you do find it.\" And this can bear two interpretations: it could be said that among the children of the World to Come there will be those whose rank has not reached the level to benefit constantly from the radiance of the Shekhinah, and their existence will be through a thing corporealized from that radiance, like the rank of the generation of the wilderness who attained the radiance of the Shekhinah at the Sea — as they said (Mekhilta above, 15:2): a maidservant saw at the Sea what Ezekiel the prophet did not see — and from that hour their souls were elevated to be sustained through its derivations, which is the manna.\n\nBut it is more correct that Scripture alludes to the words of Rabbi Elazar ben Chasma in the word \"today\": the existence of the children of the World to Come will be in the foundation of the manna, which is the supernal radiance, as they said (Berakhot 17a): there is in it neither eating nor drinking, but the righteous sit and their crowns are on their heads, and they enjoy the radiance of the Shekhinah. And the children of the World to Come will persist through their enjoyment of the radiance of the Shekhinah as they cleave to it through the crown upon their heads. And the crown is the attribute called כתר, of which Scripture says (Isaiah 28:5), <b>YHWH of Hosts shall be a crown of glory</b>, and of it is it said (Song of Songs 3:11), <b>with the crown with which his mother crowned him</b>. And they alluded thereby to the existence of the children of the World to Come, and they alluded thereby to the essence of the manna. And Scripture says (Psalms 78:23–25): <b>He commanded the skies above, and opened the doors of heaven, and rained down upon them manna to eat, and gave them grain of heaven; the bread of the mighty did man eat</b>, and likewise it says (ibid. 105:40), <b>and satisfied them with bread of heaven</b>. From this it appears that this grain was in heaven and He brought it down to them by opening its doors, and this is what I explained — that the light became corporealized, for concerning the supernal light Scripture employs this idiom: <b>the heavens opened and I saw visions of Elohim</b> (Ezekiel 1:1). Or perhaps it was already in heaven, as they said (Avot 5:6), that it was created between the suns."], :book :exodus, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 16, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:09:39.214952500Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}